The scanner has no notion of JavaScript/TypeScript regular-expression literals, so a `/*` sequence occurring INSIDE a regex literal is misread as the start of a block comment. Because a regex cannot contain a raw `*/` (the closing `/` would be escaped as `\/`, never a bare `*/`), the mistaken block comment NEVER closes — inblock=1 carries across lines to EOF and swallows every subsequent line, hiding any real agent_instruction read below it. This is a realistic, shippable, COMPILING TypeScript shape, not a contrived one: the common trailing-slash-strip idiom `path.replace(/\/*$/, '')` contains the raw two-char window `/*` (from the regex sub-pattern `\/*` = 'zero or more slashes'). Live-reproduced end-to-end on this exact branch in a clean non-allowlist .ts file (src/http/app-secret.ts, ext .ts => STRIP_HASH=0, the shippable path):
  const cleaned = path.replace(/\/*$/, '');
  const rows = db.query("select body from choros.agent_instruction where id=$1");
=> gate emits `SKIP FF-COMP-6: .../src/http/app-secret.ts ... comment-only` + overall `PASS: agent-instruction-runtime-dormant — all checks green` (exit 0). Direct scanner trace confirms the stripped output is truncated to `const cleaned = path.replace(/\` — everything after the regex's `/*`, INCLUDING the entire next line's table read, is dropped; grep finds 0 hits. The snippet is valid TS (no syntax error under tsc). This directly violates the module's OWN stated guarantee (script lines 66-68: 'Every stripping decision errs toward KEEPING text ... rather than dropping it (false negative: a real violation sails through)') — the unclosed-block swallow is exactly the false-negative direction the module claims never to take, and it is the same class of failure as R1/R2 (a common code shape hiding a real code-level hit). A contrived minimal form `const re = /\/*/; const rows = q("...agent_instruction...")` reproduces identically. Non-triggers confirmed for contrast: a regex with `//` inside (`/a\/\/b/`) still DETECTs (a raw `//` is unreachable in a regex — the first `/` closes it), and plain division `a /b/ c` DETECTs.
